Great conversation, @mcuban building on 
@DrBruggeman point, the penalties are real on physicians and asymmetrical when compared to health plans. @EdGainesIII @drdanchoi & @mass_marion are excellent resources on this topic as well & I hope they can expand & clarify on penalties against doctors when they overcharge. 

My understanding is that for 
In-network overcharging a patient for their cost sharing responsibility is a health plan contract violation. It leads to health plan network termination, unjust/gross  enrichment litigation claims, and exposure to state attorneys general and medical licensing boards action for insurance fraud. If Medicare Advantage is involved, it gets uglier as CMS can investigate administer civil monetary penalties and pursue criminal charges, and potential Medicare program exclusion.

Out-of-network overcharging patients under the No Surprises Act is illegal balance billing. It exposes physicians to civil penalties of up to $10,000 per claim, mandatory patient refunds within 30 days, loss of any good-faith protection if violations repeat, federal audits, corrective action plans, and layered state enforcement and again gross enrichment civil litigation.

Also note for Cash Pay or Self pay patients they can request Good Faith Estimates. If the final physician bill exceeds the estimate by $400 or more, the patient can invoke the federal dispute resolution process, adding another enforcement lever on doctors.

What Congress failed to do when they passed the #NoSurpisesAct was spell out enforcement for dead beat health plans that No/Low/Slow pay after they lose in arbitration, and CMS and the courts have not fixed this asymmetry through rule making and key litigation. 

We need support for the Bicameral Bipartisan bill introduced by @RepGregMurphy the No Surprises Enforcement Act. 

